{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-108010" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108010" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/52fc197612dac35373ecebf771b8444505902b5c6e00c7c55d2b21dab4809136.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6{x}^{3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108010" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-108010" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108010" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6{x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108010" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/785776cee4f82c5bfa5e86b5b1cb746b7c80f2d702fb52f67055a60bdff249ae.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{-2x}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/dd924518ae28ee8bf010e76b95ddbd68a26137b8b1f28753900455a87e1df799.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{-2x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "4{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/00da8cdff7814df7368ff00924360db24a93934c5ae1c87ceb5daa6b00df37a7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${cos}^{2}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/768db6b8051d8f4bea528f6c5b5d386ffc0d6529517d2fe72641cb33f6e8ac10.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $2\\,m$ when its velocity is $8\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=2\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=8\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =2(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =32-8 \\\\ C & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+24 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+48 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}+12) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}"] ["math" "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "v(x)=2{x}^{2}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\frac{8}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+21}"] ["math" "v(x)={x}^{2}+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.9\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "2.3\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "3.3\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/ec4af1357e75dcee6d7d5412467c2ee2ea9e54fa38fb6e83817b0ae909c529aa.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+3{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x-3{e}^{-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2(0)-3{e}^{-0}+C \\\\ 2 & =-3+C \\\\ C & =2+3 \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x-3{e}^{-x}+5 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4x-6{e}^{-x}+10 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4(3)-6{e}^{-3}+10} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{22-6{e}^{-3}} \\\\ v & ≈4.7\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-108002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3.4\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "3.8\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "4.2\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "4.7\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "5.1\\text{ m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-108002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.7\\text{ m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $3\\,m$ when its velocity is $2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/164c90893a07ae9657e90b6f2b25e53c9c4927b1f4a076429ba0521a98377f24.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={e}^{x-3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=3\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & ={e}^{3-3}+C \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({e}^{x-3}+1) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=3{e}^{x-3}-1"] ["math" "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\sqrt{3{e}^{x-3}+1}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x-3}+3}"] ["math" "v(x)={e}^{x-3}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-2.3\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-2.9\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-3.7\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=7\\,m$ and has velocity $v=12\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/634a0680d0f159006c524b8d0eb466b2c99b0f9b5a46cf38b7cd689c9d8d6fb2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=12\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=7\\,\\text{m}$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(12)^{2} & =\\frac{9(7+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ 72 & =72+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =9(x+1)^{4/3} \\\\ v & =\\pm 3(x+1)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3(x+1)^{2/3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=7$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x+1}=t+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+2 \\\\ x+1 & =(t+2)^{3} \\\\ x & =(t+2)^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=(t+1)^{3}+6"] ["math" "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=2(t+2)^{3}-9"] ["math" "x(t)=9-\\sqrt[3]{t+8}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t+8}+5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+4{x}^{2})\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/59c4a51d419b6e42633579b687cde16a2842895c589f818942c10aa304bc98a7.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2x & =t+K \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=0,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\pi t"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi +t)"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t"] ["math" "x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t-\\pi )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=(12t-1)^{3}+1"] ["math" "x(t)=(3t+2)^{3}-8"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{2t+1}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t-8}+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)={e}^{-2x}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/8faba5a783797f7025f51ec1ae587cb68355dff7138278689346c2f713275562.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{-2x}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x} & =t+K \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =2t+C \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4t+1)"] ["math" "x(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)"] ["math" "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)"] ["math" "x(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{4}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/ba9988a12d1c70c43a6a010c3578850fe79a9bca01309884fe981b3f7cca512c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2(x+2)^{3}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)^{4}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =(x+2)^{4} \\\\ v & =\\pm (x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x+2} & =t+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=-t+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1-2t}{2} \\\\ x+2 & =\\frac{2}{1-2t} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-2t}-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4t}{1-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2t-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{t}{1-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-2t}"] ["math" "x(t)=t-4{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7f667ed2e10b6efc2981260fdbeb666ba37a732871db5d968cd317b35313e26"} {"question" "e-9050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{ m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ what differential equation describes this motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/example-9050.json" "file_sha256" "cec478c2bac716059a8f47f242c2fe57577d815d620fba5883714a371814e606"} {"question" "e-9050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/f187eff194f136838b67ab12b44f8f6882eafb59885a5c3f440d41492def4a42.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/example-9050.json" "file_sha256" "cec478c2bac716059a8f47f242c2fe57577d815d620fba5883714a371814e606"} {"question" "e-9051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2.$ If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/example-9051.json" "file_sha256" "6d01285065e2e3773f5b4e066ef65941b3defd16e6056af20b0cc07460410191"} {"question" "e-9051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/4fdd70a4ca80965c0adce9c60be36b0712852b662cff69f9733f50c739fd1b32.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{e}^{x-2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{e}^{2-2}+C \\\\ 8 & =4+1+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x) \\gt 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({1}^{2}+{e}^{1-2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(4+{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & ≈2.96\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/example-9051.json" "file_sha256" "6d01285065e2e3773f5b4e066ef65941b3defd16e6056af20b0cc07460410191"} {"question" "e-9455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/example-9455.json" "file_sha256" "365cacf9cb48ad00d16a96fe61341b66bd9fa1c0bfc0cc410c78a362b50ddd00"} {"question" "e-9455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/eca36626e6a0cfb30d293e684483c37ddce6f18d4004c3f77258e9663565d8d7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=0\\, \\textrm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{2}{0+1}+K \\\\ 2 & =2+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x) > 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1)^{3/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{2/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/example-9455.json" "file_sha256" "365cacf9cb48ad00d16a96fe61341b66bd9fa1c0bfc0cc410c78a362b50ddd00"}] "usage" {"e-9050" [] "q-107999" [] "q-108002" [] "q-108010" [] "e-9455" [] "e-9051" []} "basis" 1530 "retractions" [[17592186167003 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x > 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $vm/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6x^{3}m/s^{2}$. Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O$, which differential equation describes this motion?"] [17592186167000 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $xm$, its velocity is $vm/s$ and its acceleration is $4xm/s^{2}$. If $P$ has a displacement of $2m$ when its velocity is $8m/s$, find $v$ in terms of $x$."] [17592186167001 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$, away from a fixed origin $O$. When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $xm$, its velocity is $vm/s$ and its acceleration is $(2 + 3e^{-x})m/s^{2}$. If $v = 2m/s$ when $x = 0m$, find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 3m$. Round your answer to one decimal place."] [17592186166634 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2$. Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O$, what differential equation describes this motion?"] [17592186166634 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/cd73ecc46cd29774b30dd48cc477c4d3.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dx} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} =-\\sin^{2} x\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x\n$$"] [17592186166635 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$, away from a fixed origin $O$. When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m}$, its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2$. If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m$, find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m$. Round your answer to two decimal places."] [17592186166635 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/adcc30a786d5323ec9b2c92769b6d11e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2})dx &= ∫(2x + e^{x - 2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx = 2 \\, \\textrm m\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} &= 2^{2} + e^{2 - 2} + C \\\\\n8 &= 4 + 1 + C \\\\\nC &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3 \\\\\nv^{2} &= 2(x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3) \\\\\nv &= ± \\sqrt{2(x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we have that $v(x) \\gt 0$, so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\sqrt{2(1^{2} + e^{1 - 2} + 3)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2(4 + e^{-1})} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.96m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186166644 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m$, the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O$. Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$. Find $x$ as a function of time $t$."] [17592186166644 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/10ca9059211ec926ef3e8403f61e367f.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2\n$$\n\nand is directed *toward* $O$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2})dx &= ∫ - (2)/((x + 1)^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv=2\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx=0\\, \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $K$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} + K \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} &= \\frac{2}{0 + 1} + K \\\\\n2 &= 2 + K \\\\\nK &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} \\\\\nv^{2} &= \\frac{4}{x + 1} \\\\\nv &= ± \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we have that $v(x) > 0$, so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nv= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 1}} \\\\\n\\sqrt{x + 1}\\frac{dx}{dt} &= 2 \\\\\n∫\\sqrt{x + 1}\\frac{dx}{dt}dt &= ∫2dt \\\\\n∫\\sqrt{x + 1}dx &= ∫2dt \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 2t + C_{1} \\\\\n(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 3t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0$. Therefore, $C=1$, and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 3t + 1 \\\\\nx + 1 &= (3t + 1)^{2/3} \\\\\nx &= (3t + 1)^{2/3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163419 17592186163420 17592186163421 17592186163422 17592186163423 17592186163425 17592186163426 17592186163427 17592186163428 17592186163429 17592186163437 17592186163438 17592186163439 17592186163440 17592186163441] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163418 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163418 :answer-field/id] [17592186163418 :answer-field/key] [17592186163418 :answer-field/type] [17592186163424 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163424 :answer-field/id] [17592186163424 :answer-field/key] [17592186163424 :answer-field/type] [17592186163436 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163436 :answer-field/id] [17592186163436 :answer-field/key] [17592186163436 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
